\begin{abstract}
\rev{Unsupervised analysis of the electrocardiogram (ECG) promises label-free discovery of cardiac structure. How well clustering recovers clinical classes from each common family of signal representation, hand-crafted features among them, has not been compared under chance-corrected metrics. We benchmarked four representation families for unsupervised separation of sinus rhythm (SR) from atrial fibrillation (AF) on PTB-XL. The families were whole-record raw flattening, a convolutional autoencoder (conv-AE), compact hand-crafted rhythm and morphology features, and two frozen foundation-model embeddings (HuBERT-ECG and ECGFounder). Evaluation was chance-corrected (adjusted Rand index, ARI) and patient-grouped, so that no patient appeared in more than one fold. It was repeated over ten seeds for the clusterers that depend on one. Each representation was bounded by a supervised ceiling, the accuracy a supervised classifier reaches on the same features. ECGFounder clustered best (ARI up to 0.885 with $k$-means), and 16 interpretable hand-crafted features were competitive (0.711 with agglomerative clustering). The raw and conv-AE representations matched a majority-class assignment (ARI $\approx 0$), although the conv-AE reached a supervised ceiling of 0.678 against a majority baseline of 0.504. The raw ceiling was 0.519, barely above that baseline. The same ordering of the representations held on a MIT-BIH beat-morphology task. The silhouette score rewarded the degenerate one-cluster solution (silhouette 0.81 at ARI 0.000), so it cannot validate ECG clustering alone. Feature scaling also changed the outcome: the mean ARI of the hand-crafted features fell from 0.629 under standard scaling to 0.229 under quantile scaling. These chance-corrected controls, which locate where the raw and conv-AE representations fail, are the contribution, together with the measurement of each representation under deployment stress. Under heavy class imbalance the hand-crafted features overtook ECGFounder. Under federation across ten heterogeneous sites the $k$-means ARI of the hand-crafted features fell from 0.644 to 0.591, while that of ECGFounder fell from 0.885 to 0.612. The extraction of the hand-crafted features cost fifteen times less per record than that of ECGFounder.}
\end{abstract}

\rev{We therefore benchmarked four representation families for unsupervised SR-versus-AF separation (sinus rhythm versus atrial fibrillation), with the controls the original project lacked. The ordering of the representations follows from the class definitions and is not the contribution. SR and AF differ by definition in the regularity of the rhythm, so it is expected that compact hand-crafted features and a large foundation model both recover the split. The benchmark adds what its controls reveal and how the representations behave under deployment stress. The contributions are empirical:}
\begin{revblock}
\begin{enumerate}
\item A clustering benchmark of raw, convolutional autoencoder (conv-AE), hand-crafted and two frozen foundation-model representations. It is chance-corrected and significance-tested, and it is patient-grouped: every record of a patient stays in one fold. A supervised classifier trained on each representation sets its supervised ceiling, against which its clustering is read. The ordering is shown stable across the assumed number of clusters.
\item Two reproducible methodological cautions that explain the original project's misleading numbers. Internal validity indices, which score a partition from the data alone without labels, reward the degenerate one-cluster solution. Feature scaling, the per-feature transformation applied before clustering, changes the result by a wide margin.
\item A deployment comparison under two independent stressors: heavy class imbalance, and federation across sites that differ sharply in arrhythmia prevalence. The 16 hand-crafted features overtake ECGFounder, the stronger foundation model on balanced data, under imbalance. Across the federated sites they keep a larger share of the adjusted Rand index (ARI) they reach without federation than either foundation model does.
\item A generalization check on a MIT-BIH beat-morphology task, the dataset the closest prior benchmark omits.
\end{enumerate}
\end{revblock}
\rev{We propose no new method, and we credit the separability-versus-clusterability phenomenon to prior work. Every statement about a foundation model is scoped to the two models tested, for the reasons given in Section~\ref{sec:discussion}.}

\rev{We compared four representation families on PTB-XL (Table~\ref{tab:main}):}
\begin{revblock}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Raw flattening} reproduced the original project's design on all twelve leads, since that design concatenated every lead. Each lead of the 1000-by-12 signal was reduced by a non-overlapping six-sample mean to 166 samples, and the leads were concatenated into one 1992-dimensional vector ($\sim$2000 dimensions). Its features are the sample amplitudes themselves, with no clinical model imposed on the signal. It carries amplitude and energy, and nothing aligns the samples of one record in time with those of another.
\item \textbf{Convolutional autoencoder} (conv-AE): a 1-D convolutional autoencoder with a 32-unit bottleneck, trained to convergence with a validation split, on all twelve leads resampled to 12 by 2500 and z-scored per lead. This is a properly specified reconstruction model, in contrast to the original collapsed dense autoencoder. Because its objective is reconstruction, the bottleneck keeps whatever carries signal energy, which need not be what separates the classes.
\item \textbf{Hand-crafted} (16 features): RR-interval statistics and irregularity, P-wave presence and amplitude, QRS width and amplitude, and beat-template consistency \cite{hrvstandards1996,dechazal2004}, extracted with NeuroKit2 \cite{neurokit2021}. The RR statistics were SDNN (the standard deviation of the RR intervals), RMSSD (the root mean square of successive RR differences), pNN50 (the proportion of successive RR differences above \mbox{50\,ms}), the coefficient of variation, RR entropy and mean heart rate. All 16 features were computed from the 1000 samples of lead II alone, on which R-peak detection and wave delineation were performed. Lead II is the standard rhythm lead, the one on which RR regularity and P-wave presence are read. Automated AF detection from a single short ECG lead was also the task of the PhysioNet/Computing in Cardiology Challenge 2017 \cite{clifford2017af}. The representation measures directly the two quantities that make up the AF signature, RR irregularity and P-wave absence.
\item \textbf{Foundation embeddings} (frozen): mean-pooled hidden states of HuBERT-ECG (768-d) \cite{hubertecg2024} and the 1024-d pooled features of ECGFounder \cite{ecgfounder2024}, both used as fixed feature extractors with no fine-tuning. Both took all twelve leads, the input of the 12-lead versions of the published models. HuBERT-ECG received the first \mbox{5\,s} at \mbox{100\,Hz}, flattened lead-major into a 6000-sample vector. ECGFounder received the full strip resampled to 12 by 5000 for its \mbox{500\,Hz} input, z-scored once over the whole signal. This arm asks whether a feature space fixed by large-scale pretraining places the rhythm classes in separate regions.
\end{itemize}
\end{revblock}
\rev{On MIT-BIH the rhythm features do not transfer to single beats, so a raw beat window, a 21-dimensional beat-morphology and RR-context feature set, and the conv-AE were compared. The 21-dimensional set is a separate beat-level feature set of the same kind as the 16 PTB-XL features, not the same features. Neither foundation model was applied to the 2-lead beats. The versions used take a 12-lead strip of several seconds, and each MIT-BIH input is a 2-lead window around one R-peak. The two lead sets of the PTB-XL arms answer to different constraints. The hand-crafted arm was single-lead by clinical convention, and the raw arm was twelve-lead because that is the design it reproduces. The raw arm therefore received more input than the hand-crafted arm, so a lack of input is unlikely to explain its chance-level clustering. The twelve-lead input also made the raw vector 1992-dimensional, however, and that dimensionality may itself bear on Euclidean clustering.}

